# Device class-map — copy to /etc/dashboard/classmap and edit for your devices.
#
# It gives each device a readable name and an icon in the client list. Devices
# missing from this file still appear; they are just labelled by hostname or MAC
# and get a generic icon. Nothing here affects routing, DHCP, or firewalling.
#
# Pipe-delimited, keyed by MAC (uppercase):
#
#   MAC | name | class | last_known_ip | network
#
#   class    one of: computer phone pad laptop television watch smartappliances
#            (the icon set the dashboard draws from)
#   network  the UCI network the device belongs on — lan, or the name of your
#            guest/IoT network if you run one. Optional: leave it empty and the
#            dashboard works it out from the device's address.
#
# last_known_ip is informational only — a reminder of where the device usually
# sits. DHCP may hand out something else; add a static lease if you need it
# pinned.
#
# The addresses below are examples. Use whatever your router actually serves.
#
00:11:22:33:44:55|My Laptop|laptop|192.168.1.100|lan
00:11:22:33:44:66|My Phone|phone|192.168.1.101|lan
00:11:22:33:44:77|Smart Plug|smartappliances||iot
